{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: chat-composed views (the widget builder). Write notes/2026-10-20-chat-composed-views.md.\n\nA person asks a question in an AI chat. The model writes a small view declaration over the room's existing projections and acts. The host checks it and shows it in the existing view kit. A person can keep a view in the room and share it. Composing a view needs no new definitions, projections or acts.\n\nDecisions (Hugh, 2026-10-20), which the note records:\n\n1. Two layers.\n   - Compose: one model-visible tool, bound to the existing kit resource. Its input is a view declaration. The host runs the same checks it runs on package views. Acts stay server-held and are checked again before signing. Button labels come from the package's act declarations.\n   - Keep: a keep act stores the declaration in the room as an overlay on the installation. It opens by name through one fixed opener tool. Sharing needs a grant. Promotion into a package release goes through normal review.\n   - The tool list and the resource list never change at runtime.\n   - A code fallback in a nested frame is out of scope. Record it as later work, with its open host question.\n2. First kinds: line, bar, scatter, plan and geographic map. Tables, single values and forms stay with chat text, the carousel and the input tools.\n3. Every kind shows live data, with an \"as of\" time and stale marking. Tapping a mark opens that item's card through the declared opens map.\n4. Plan positions come from item fields: bounded ints, bounded decimals or ordered enums. The package presentation may name position pairs and supply an outline. That is a presentation change only. Grow's bench row and column are the test case: a plan replaces the room grid surface without package code.\n5. The geographic map is part of this work. It adds a location field type to the contract, with its bounds and scale. By default the basemap makes no third-party requests. The note decides between bundled outlines and a tile domain declared in the resource policy, and states the size cost.\n6. Filters are structured only: a field, an operator and a value. Operators include relative time. Filters compile to restricted@1.\n7. Aggregation runs in the host's view session. Views receive buckets, not raw rows, within the 64 KiB mount bound.\n8. Spec rules:\n   - Use our own kinds, with channels x, y, color and label.\n   - Name sources and fields only; give no data and no types.\n   - Use a flat list of blocks, and refuse unknown properties.\n   - Allow at most 4 series, and include a version field.\n   - Return all errors at once. Each error has a code, a JSON Pointer, a message and the allowed values.\n   - Allow two repair rounds.\n   - Provide a catalogue tool that returns field types, enum values, act parameters, a default kind for the data's shape, and one worked example per kind.\n9. The renderer is our own SVG code within the existing resource budget. It uses no eval.\n10. The note names its acceptance check: a fixed set of room questions, the first-pass validity rate, the rate after repair, and a reviewed sample for wrong charts.\n11. Evidence: the note cites research findings with their evidence labels (measured, survey, catalog, proxy, estimate, opinion) and names no products. The research stays outside the repository, in ~/play/dap-widget-research (00-synthesis.md, then 01 to 04). The checker may read it locally.\n12. This request covers the document only. A checker review comes before landing. Implementation requests follow after the note lands.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no product names in the note; implementation requests follow after it lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}